          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is a VLCC?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A VLCC, or very large crude carrier, is the largest class of oil tanker in regular service. It carries about two million barrels of crude oil, is about 1,100 feet long, and is the workhorse of the long-haul trade from the Middle East to Asia."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What does a VLCC day rate mean?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The day rate is what the ship earns per day after paying for fuel and port fees, spread over a full round trip, ballast leg included. Brokers call it the time charter equivalent. The figure quoted in the news, and in this chart, is the Baltic Exchange's assessment for the TD3C route from Ras Tanura in Saudi Arabia to Ningbo in China."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much does it cost to ship a barrel of oil from the Middle East to China right now?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "In mid-September 2026, about $8 to $11.50 a barrel for a supertanker loading just outside the Strait of Hormuz, which is how most Gulf crude is moving, and $18.60 to $22 a barrel at the headline rate for a ship loading inside the strait. War-risk insurance comes on top. In a normal year the trip costs $1 to $2 a barrel."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often is this chart updated?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Every week. The Baltic Exchange publishes its weekly tanker report on Fridays, and the chart is updated when the new rate is out."
          }
        }
      ]
